    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Overview and Motivation\n\nModern large models exceed the memory and compute capacity of a single accelerator. Two complementary parallelism strategies address this during inference:\n\n- **Tensor Parallelism (TP)**: Splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a shard of every layer and processes the *same* tokens.\n- **Expert Parallelism (EP)**: Splits the *experts* of a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layer across devices. Each device holds a few complete experts; *different* tokens are routed to different devices.\n\nIn production inference stacks (vLLM, TensorRT-LLM, SGLang, DeepSpeed-Inference, Megatron-style serving), TP and EP are frequently combined: dense/attention layers use TP, MoE layers use EP (sometimes with TP-within-expert), and the whole thing is replicated across nodes via data parallelism or pipeline parallelism.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 What gets sharded\n\nFor a transformer layer, the dominant matrices are the attention projections (QKV, output projection) and the MLP up/down projections. Two canonical sharding schemes exist:\n\n**Row-parallel (split along the input dimension):**\n- Weight matrix `W \u2208 \u211d^{d_out \u00d7 d_in}` is split along `d_in`: device *i* holds `W_i \u2208 \u211d^{d_out \u00d7 d_in/N}`.\n- The input activation `x \u2208 \u211d^{d_in}` must also be split: device *i* receives `x_i \u2208 \u211d^{d_in/N}`.\n- Each device computes a **partial** output: `y_i = W_i \u00b7 x_i \u2208 \u211d^{d_out}`.\n- The full output requires a **reduction** across devices.\n\n**Column-parallel (split along the output dimension):**\n- Device *i*",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
